review.changes (added, matchLevel function).","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"requirement":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y"},"type":"requirement_affected"},"detail":"Authored by our proof work (3609ba52). It is `observed_pass` (3 passed) and its error_handling cell is `observed_pass`. Witnesses: a link root plus its target, and a root given twice, each give one `clip.webm` row; a missing path next to a real root is refused with no partial listing. REVIEW-261002-VZ1K covers the defensive row. The rationale says the key is the file, not the path, so hard links also merge. It states plainly that the PR gives the intent for aliased roots only. Status `review` / `in_review`.","refs":[{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-test.sh","kind":"file","line":105},{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55}],"severity":"medium","title":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y (added): a file reached through two given paths is listed once, keyed on device:inode","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y","kind":"requirement"}],"method":"review.changes (spec, code-trace, function and call-level requirement impact)","note":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y is in review.changes (added, matchLevel function).","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"requirement":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29"},"type":"requirement_affected"},"detail":"The script changed under an unchanged HR29. `observed_pass` (2 passed). Its error_handling cell (inherited from SYS-REQ-260922-6642) is now `observed_pass` via the new `refuse()` rows. Its own external_call_timeout_bounded cell is `not_linked`; the package lists it as accepted KI debt (KI-MENU-SELECT-POLL-DEADLOCK), which pre-dates this PR. The trace re-review restamped HR29 (18f775c9, our proof work). review.changes lists external_call_timeout_bounded under acceptedKiDebt.","refs":[{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-test.sh","kind":"file","line":121}],"severity":"info","title":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29 (code_changed, preserved): formats, dotfiles, mtime order, missing-path rejection","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29","kind":"requirement"}],"method":"review.changes (spec, code-trace, function and call-level requirement impact)","note":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29 is in review.changes (code_changed, matchLevel function).","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file"],"scenario":"omarchy-menu-file \"Pick\" \"$root\" \"jpg\" where $root/a/x.jpg and $root/b/x.jpg are hard links to one inode: base offers both paths, head offers one"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"`awk -F'\\t' '!seen[$1]++'` on `%D:%i` keys on the file, not the root alias. Two distinct paths that are hard links to one inode (e.g. a photo hard-linked into two album folders) were two rows at base. At head they are one row, and the other path cannot be picked. Codified now, as the code behaves: 9H5Y's rationale and hazard review (edge_case) and REVIEW-261002-VZ1K state it. Still only a question: the PR states the intent for aliased roots only, no upstream text says hiding a hard-linked path is wanted, and no test runs a hard link.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"medium","title":"Question: is it intended that hard-linked files collapse to one picker row?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Hard-linked files under one root\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file","bin/omarchy-transcode"],"scenario":"omarchy-transcode with ~/Videos a symlink to a tree containing a mode-000 subdirectory: the user picks a file, and omarchy-menu-file exits 1 under pipefail so omarchy-transcode aborts"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"Under `set -euo pipefail` the pipeline status is the rightmost non-zero one. `find -H ... 2>/dev/null` exits 1 on Permission denied but still prints the readable rows. The selector returns the pick, the script exits 1, and `bin/omarchy-transcode:169` (`input=$(omarchy-menu-file ...)` under `set -e`) aborts after the pick. This already happens for a real-directory root at base. -H makes it reachable through link roots (e.g. `~/Videos -> /mnt/disk` with a root-owned `lost+found`), which listed nothing at base. Not codified as a guarantee: FFTQ's hazard review and REVIEW-261002-6P46 name it explicitly as open and not covered by the requirement. The PR's interior-loop row covers loops, not permissions.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-transcode","kind":"file","line":169},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"medium","title":"Question: can a pick succeed and the script still exit 1 when a followed symlink root contains an unreadable directory?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Unreadable subdirectory under a followed symlink root\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file"],"scenario":"two files with identical mtime under one root, and aliased roots Pictures->Videos: compare row order and the surviving path spelling at base and head"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"Base `sort -rn` reverses the whole-line tiebreak, so equal-mtime rows were ordered by path descending. Head `sort -t$'\\t' -k2,2rn` makes `r` apply to the key only, so ties fall back to an ascending whole-line comparison that starts with `D:i`. Re-checked with GNU sort on synthetic rows: `9:1 Pictures/b` is kept over `9:1 Videos/b`, and base orders `/r/b` before `/r/a`. The full find pipeline was not run (no GNU find on this host). Partly codified: REVIEW-261002-VZ1K says the lexically smaller line is kept, and 9H5Y's hazard review says the spelling depends on the sort order of equal rows. HR29 states mtime-descending only. No test pins the spelling the picker returns or the tie order.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Question: which alias spelling survives the dedup, and is the new equal-mtime order wanted?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Equal-mtime ordering and surviving alias spelling\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file"],"scenario":"omarchy-menu-file \"Pick\" \"$tmp/link.jpg\" \"jpg\" where link.jpg is a symlink to a real jpg: base offers no row, head offers one"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"`-H` dereferences every start point. A root that is a link to a regular file with a matching extension was `-type l` at base (not listed). At head it is `-type f` and listed as one row. `[[ -e $path ]]` accepts it either way. Codified as the code behaves in FFTQ's hazard review (edge_case). FFTQ's description still speaks of links to directories only. The PR describes directory roots only. No test covers it.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":32},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Question: is a symlink-to-file root meant to be listed now?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Symlink-to-file root\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"detail":"review.compat is `compatible`: test_diff, mcdc_diff (85 -> 87 decisions), removed_behavior and differential were all checked, with 0 findings and 213 inputs over 5 partitions. The configured harness `test/bdiff/harness.mjs` covers only `shell/plugins/menu/MenuModel.js` and `Menu.qml`, so none of those inputs ran this script. `review.behaviorDiff.inventory` is empty. The removed expressions (`find` default -P, `-printf '%T@\\t%p\\n'`, `sort -rn`, `cut -f2-`) were never run base-vs-head; the hazards above are what such a run would settle. test_diff reports no existing test changed. The edits to `menu-file-test.sh` (our proof work) only add rows and widen its Verifies line. Context, not an approval.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":47},{"id":"test/bdiff/harness.mjs","kind":"file"}],"severity":"info","title":"Compatibility 'compatible' carries no differential evidence for bin/omarchy-menu-file","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"`coverage_unmeasured`: `review.diffCoverage.unmeasuredFiles` = [bin/omarchy-menu-file]. The 0 uncovered lines/outcomes says nothing about lines 47-56. `effects_unavailable`: `review.effects` is absent (call-level effects are not analyzed for bash), so no new-call, gained-effect or caller facts exist. Callers found by reading: `bin/omarchy-transcode:169` and `bin/omarchy-games-retro-install:22` (`|| exit 0`).","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-games-retro-install","kind":"file","line":22}],"severity":"info","title":"Changed-code coverage of the script was not measured; effects were not analyzed","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"The script's two self-skips (`no GNU find`, `find lacks -printf`) call base-test.sh `pass` (not `skip`) and exit 0. The JUnit testcase would therefore record a pass with nothing run. On this run the evidence points to a real run. `menu-file-test.sh` sits in the same suite behind a GNU find -printf gate that records a JUnit skip (proof.yaml:200), and it ran and passed: testResults show 56/56 passed, 0 skipped. Its new witness rows independently exercise FFTQ and 9H5Y. Upstream could use `skip` (`# SKIP`), which test/junit/live-session.sh already treats as a skip.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh","kind":"file","line":11},{"id":"proof.yaml","kind":"file","line":200},{"id":"test/junit/live-session.sh","kind":"file","line":129}],"severity":"info","title":"The PR's test passes on a self-skip too (opinion)","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"The body still says 'Dedup rows after `cut -f2-` with `awk '!seen[$0]++'`'. Head 03e6af6 keys on `%D:%i` before `cut -f3-`. Worth updating so the merged history describes the shipped mechanism, including the hard-link consequence.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":52}],"severity":"info","title":"PR description no longer matches the head (opinion)","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"`-printf` was already GNU-only, and `%D`/`%i` are also GNU (both test scripts gate on GNU find). The `checks_skipped` disclosure (13 gates, e.g. documentation_coverage, security_surface_covered) is configuration, not caused by this PR. Any external auditor assessment recorded on this package is advisory: a note, not an approval.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh","kind":"file","line":10},{"id":"documentation_coverage","kind":"check"}],"severity":"info","title":"Pre-existing: GNU find dependency; 13 checks skipped by configuration","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}}],"packageDigest":"sha256:fdce43b19861b23cc393652b55097a144315b1651c96d19781b345e647b14e07","packageIdentity":"epkg-bbf53bc2859a863757f78cc96228e234c575b8b161e818e87692dedaf9be30a8","riskAssessment":{"level":"medium","rationale":"A small, well-motivated pipeline change. Its purpose is now codified (FFTQ, 9H5Y) and observed passing, with witnesses that fail on the base. Coverage of the script was not measured, effects were not analyzed, and the differential harness does not run the script. Untested edges remain: hard-link collapse, pipefail exit 1 via an unreadable directory under a followed root (named open in FFTQ), tie order and alias spelling, symlink-to-file roots."},"schemaVersion":"reqproof.review-annotation.v1","suggestedTests":[{"forClaim":4,"rationale":"Settles whether %D:%i dedup should drop a distinct, selectable path; record the decision in 9H5Y or the code.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55}],"title":"Hard-linked files under one root"},{"forClaim":5,"rationale":"mkdir a mode-000 dir under the link target (run as non-root), pick a file with a stub selector, and assert omarchy-menu-file's exit status (and omarchy-transcode's).","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-transcode","kind":"file","line":169}],"title":"Unreadable subdirectory under a followed symlink root"},{"forClaim":6,"rationale":"touch -d the same time on two files and on an aliased pair, then assert row order and which path the stub selector receives, at base and head.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55}],"title":"Equal-mtime ordering and surviving alias spelling"},{"forClaim":7,"rationale":"Pass a symlink to a matching file as the path argument; assert whether it is offered.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54}],"title":"Symlink-to-file root"}],"summary":"**What the PR does.** Upstream #13197 (Bartok9, part of #12665) makes `bin/omarchy-menu-file` list files under symlinked roots, so `omarchy transcode` gets a picker when `~/Videos` is a link. It runs `find -H` (start points followed, links inside a root not descended), prints `%D:%i`, and keeps one row per device:inode after the mtime sort. It adds `test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh` (8 rows). The mirror (tip dedfc493, an empty record commit on quattro-proof 0c61a551) applies it as 59b1e7d9. Our proof work (18f775c9..199b05fd) adds SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ and SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y under SYS-REQ-260922-6642, MC/DC and error_handling witnesses in `menu-file-test.sh`, conformance reviews REVIEW-261002-6P46/-VZ1K, and a `# Verifies:` line on the PR's test. The PR body is stale: it still describes a path-string dedup.\n\n**Is the fix verified?** The package says readiness `ready`; the next decision is `human_judgement` (open_judgements) for one open spec-gap candidate. FFTQ, 9H5Y and HR29 are `observed_pass`; their error_handling cells are `observed_pass`; HR29's external_call_timeout_bounded is `not_linked` (accepted KI debt, KI-MENU-SELECT-POLL-DEADLOCK). testResults: 56 passed, 0 failed, current. Coverage of the script was not measured (`coverage_unmeasured`) and effects were not analyzed (`effects_unavailable`). Compatibility is `compatible` (213 inputs, 0 findings), but the differential harness never runs this script.\n\n**Questions for a human.** Is it intended that hard links collapse to one row? Can a pick under a followed root holding an unreadable directory still exit 1 under pipefail? Which alias spelling survives, and is the new equal-mtime order wanted? Is a symlink-to-file root meant to be listed? The requirements now record the first, third and fourth as the code behaves and name